## 🔄 Your Understanding (Corrected)

> *"We are training on dataset-2 and for labeling each image we are seeing the dataset-4 right and we are filtering the images from the NCR Region"*

**Yes, you're mostly right. Here's the precise flow:**

1. **Dataset 2 (RGB images)** = the input images we classify
2. **Dataset 4 (land cover TIF)** = used to generate **labels** for each image (not used for training directly, only for label extraction)
3. **Filtering** = we take all 9216 images and keep only those whose center coordinate falls **inside the Delhi-NCR boundary** (Dataset 1), **NOT** the airshed
4. **Dataset 3 (Airshed)** is only for visualization/context — the grid overlay and understanding the study area

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The filtering uses **Dataset 1 (NCR boundary)**, not Dataset 3 (Airshed). The code does `gpd.sjoin(img_gdf, ncr_4326, how='inner', predicate='within')` — spatial join against NCR.

